Write a brief that the rewriting steps will work from. The rewrite is free to change how this prompt is expressed but has to keep everything it actually requires, so the brief's job is to make those requirements explicit enough that a later revision can be checked against them.

Cover, in this order:

Task — one or two sentences on what this prompt asks a model to do, and what a good response to it looks like. Add what the prompt reveals about where it sits: who or what fills its placeholders, and what consumes the answer. Mark anything you are inferring as an inference.

Requirements — every requirement and constraint the prompt expresses, one per line, numbered R1, R2, and so on. Include the ones it only implies: an output format its wording takes for granted, a tone, a length, an audience, something it rules out. Where the prompt is ambiguous, say so and give the reading you think was intended. Keep this list to what the prompt actually demands — what it leaves open belongs under Open decisions. Later steps treat this list as settled, so a flagged uncertainty is worth far more than a confident guess.

