Examples — each worked example the prompt contains, what it demonstrates, and whether it is there to fix an output format, a style, or an edge case. Write "none" if the prompt has no examples.

Weaknesses — what in the prompt as written is unclear, redundant, contradictory, or likely to produce inconsistent results, each with the specific text at fault. What the prompt never says at all belongs under Open decisions instead.

Open decisions — what the prompt leaves unsettled that the rewrite will have to settle: a format it never names, a length it never bounds, an audience it never identifies, a definition of done it never gives. For each, say what is missing and what a sensible default would be. These are choices, not requirements — a later step must not treat one as something to preserve.

Judgment — where this task asks the answering model to weigh something rather than follow a rule: what pulls against what, and where answers to this prompt are most likely to go wrong. Name what is worth weighing. Do not prescribe an order to weigh it in, and do not propose a scratchpad, a planning section or a step-by-step procedure — the rewritten prompt is for a model that reasons on its own before it answers.

